I wondered that, too.
我也一直很好奇。

1244
00:53:12.080 --> 00:53:18.160
Phil Shea wrote a letter, and I was very grateful because as I read it, it helped me
菲尔·谢写了一封信，我非常感激，因为当我读它的时候，它帮我

1245
00:53:18.160 --> 00:53:23.320
get in a headspace because there were all these things I read, but then I knew I was going
进入了状态，因为我读到了上面写的所有东西，但我知道我接下来要

1246
00:53:23.320 --> 00:53:27.600
to tell Michael the scripted lines of, like, it's not over.
对迈克尔说剧本里的台词，比如，还没结束。

1247
00:53:28.460 --> 00:53:29.380
And it was great.
那感觉太棒了。

1248
00:53:29.640 --> 00:53:33.800
It was so helpful as an actor to have not just a blank screen there.
作为演员，能不只是对着空白屏幕演戏，真的非常有帮助。

1249
00:53:33.940 --> 00:53:35.860
So thank you, Phil Shea, for writing me a letter.
所以谢谢你，菲尔·谢，给我写了那封信。

1250
00:53:36.060 --> 00:53:40.020
I wonder if Phil had to consult the writing staff and say, guys, I'm going to write this
我在想菲尔是不是得去咨询编剧组，说，伙计们，我要写这个内容了

1251
00:53:40.020 --> 00:53:40.360
letter.
信件。

1252
00:53:40.540 --> 00:53:40.960
Do you have any?
你有吗？

1253
00:53:41.120 --> 00:53:42.600
Or he just did that on his own?
还是他自己那么做的？

1254
00:53:42.820 --> 00:53:44.720
I think that was stuff he just did on his own.
我觉得那是他自己加的戏。

1255
00:53:44.840 --> 00:53:48.220
He was a very detail-oriented prop master.
他是一位非常注重细节的道具师。

1256
